# No database queries in loops

Never call the database (repository methods, `manager.*`, `QueryBuilder`, `dataSource.query`, or any service that queries) once per item inside a loop. A loop of N queries becomes N round trips and a slow request. This includes `for`, `for ... of`, `while`, `forEach`, and `map` with `await` inside.

## Use one query instead

- **Insert many rows:** build the entities and save the array once.

```ts
// BAD
for (const master of masters) {
  saved.push(await types.save(types.create({ ...toRow(master) })));
}

// GOOD
return types.save(masters.map((master) => types.create({ ...toRow(master) })));
```

- **Update many rows to different values:** one `UPDATE ... CASE`. Put it in a named repository method (see `backend-data-access.mdc`).

```ts
// BAD
for (const item of dto.items) {
  await types.update({ id: item.id, organizationId }, { sortOrder: item.sortOrder });
}

// GOOD (repository method)
await types.updateSortOrders(organizationId, dto.items, userId);
```

- **Read many rows:** one `find` with `In(ids)` (or `IN (:...ids)`), then look up in memory with a `Map`.
- **Update many rows to the same value:** one `update({ id: In(ids) }, { ... })`.

## What is not allowed

- Not inside a loop, even inside a transaction. A transaction does not make a loop of queries acceptable.
- Not `Promise.all` over repository calls as a workaround. That still issues N queries.

## If a loop seems required

Check first whether a batch save, `IN` query, or `CASE` update covers it. If you still think a per-item query is needed, stop and ask the user before writing it, and explain why one query can't work.

## Not covered

- Loops over in-memory data, HTTP calls to other services, and LLM or image generation are not database queries and are not covered by this rule.
